1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:17,323
This is MATH 239, video 27. The topic is Hall's Theorem, perfect matchings and edge colouring. It is sections 8.4 to 8.8 of the graph theory notes, pages 197 to 206.

2
00:00:17,773 --> 00:00:39,725
After this video, you can show that no matching saturates A, and prove Hall's Theorem. You can turn a system of distinct representatives into a matching. You can prove that a regular bipartite graph has a perfect matching. And you can edge colour a bipartite graph with delta colours, and use it to plan a timetable.

3
00:00:40,175 --> 00:00:57,007
In a bipartite graph with bipartition A, B, every matching edge has one end in A. So no matching has more than A edges. We want to know when a matching of exactly this size exists. Such a matching saturates every vertex of A.

4
00:00:57,457 --> 00:01:03,131
Take the job graph again. Look at the set D equal to a, b, d, f, in red.

5
00:01:03,581 --> 00:01:13,907
The neighbour set N of D is the set of vertices joined by an edge to some vertex of D. The red edges show it. N of D is h, i, l.

6
00:01:14,357 --> 00:01:24,725
A matching that saturates D has four edges at D. They need four different ends, all in N of D. But N of D has only three vertices.

7
00:01:25,175 --> 00:01:36,033
So no matching saturates all of D. Thus no matching saturates all of A. This agrees with König: the maximum matching has size 5, not 6.

8
00:01:36,483 --> 00:01:53,209
Hall's Theorem says that this is the only thing that can go wrong. A bipartite graph G with bipartition A, B has a matching that saturates every vertex of A if and only if every subset D of A has at least as many neighbours as elements.

9
00:01:53,659 --> 00:02:00,720
First, the easy direction. Suppose a matching M saturates A. Here is a graph with such a matching.

10
00:02:01,170 --> 00:02:11,325
Take any subset D of A. Here D is 1, 3, 4. Each vertex of D has one edge of M. The other end of that edge is in N of D.

11
00:02:11,775 --> 00:02:20,628
M is a matching, so these other ends are all different. So N of D has at least D vertices. Here N of D is b, c, d.

12
00:02:21,078 --> 00:02:30,763
Now the hard direction. We prove the contrapositive. If no matching saturates A, then some subset D of A has fewer than D neighbours.

13
00:02:31,213 --> 00:02:48,408
By König's Theorem, there is a maximum matching M and a minimum cover C of the same size. No matching saturates A, so this size is less than A. The figure uses the graph of Figure 8.6, with the cover 3, 4, b, e.

14
00:02:48,858 --> 00:03:02,533
The sets A, B and C cut the vertices into four parts. C is a cover. So no edge joins A minus C to B minus C. So every neighbour of A minus C is in B intersect C.

15
00:03:02,983 --> 00:03:09,361
Let D be A minus C. Then N of D has at most as many vertices as B intersect C.

16
00:03:09,811 --> 00:03:14,078
B intersect C has C minus A intersect C vertices.

17
00:03:14,528 --> 00:03:26,069
C is smaller than A. So this is less than A minus A intersect C, which is the size of A minus C. That is the size of D. So N of D is smaller than D.

18
00:03:26,519 --> 00:03:39,469
In the example, D is 1, 2, 5. N of D has 2 vertices, B intersect C has 2, 4 minus 2 is 2, and 5 minus 2 is 3. So 2 is less than 3.

19
00:03:39,919 --> 00:03:52,569
The matching algorithm also finds such a set D. When it stops, the cover is Y hat union A minus X hat. So A minus C is X hat, and its neighbour set is Y hat.

20
00:03:53,019 --> 00:04:08,806
In video 26 b we ran the algorithm on Figure 8.6. At the stop, X hat was 1, 2, 5 and Y hat was b, e. So D is 1, 2, 5. It has three vertices but only two neighbours.

21
00:04:09,256 --> 00:04:17,661
A brute force check over all subsets of A finds every set with too few neighbours. The set 1, 2, 5 is one of them.

22
00:04:18,111 --> 00:04:27,989
So the algorithm always gives a certificate. Either it finds a matching that saturates A, or it finds a set D with fewer neighbours than elements.

23
00:04:28,439 --> 00:04:51,031
Hall first studied this problem. We have subsets Q 1 to Q n of a finite set Q. A system of distinct representatives, or S D R, is a sequence q 1 to q n of distinct elements, with q i in Q i for each i. Each set gets its own representative, and no element represents two sets.

24
00:04:51,481 --> 00:05:04,430
Here is the first example of the notes. Q 1 is b, d. Q 2 is a, b, c, d. Q 3 is b, c. Q 4 is b, d. Each row of the figure is one set.

25
00:05:04,880 --> 00:05:07,589
Choose b for Q 1 and a for Q 2.

26
00:05:08,039 --> 00:05:15,698
Then c for Q 3 and d for Q 4. All four are different. So b, a, c, d is an S D R.

27
00:05:16,148 --> 00:05:23,721
A brute force search finds exactly two S D Rs here: b, a, c, d and d, a, c, b.

28
00:05:24,171 --> 00:05:29,505
The second example changes Q 3 to the set b. The other sets stay the same.

29
00:05:29,955 --> 00:05:36,568
Look at Q 1, Q 3 and Q 4. Together they contain only two elements, b and d.

30
00:05:37,018 --> 00:05:51,503
These three sets need three different representatives, but only two elements are available. So there is no S D R. A brute force search agrees. The only bad group of sets is Q 1, Q 3, Q 4.

31
00:05:51,953 --> 00:06:08,103
Now turn the sets into a bipartite graph. A is the set of indices, 1 to n. B is the set Q. Join index k to element b if b is in Q k. This is Figure 8.10, for the first example.

32
00:06:08,553 --> 00:06:25,683
An S D R gives the edges 1 q 1, 2 q 2, and so on. The q i are distinct, so these edges form a matching that saturates A. The reverse is also true. The blue matching is the S D R b, a, c, d.

33
00:06:26,133 --> 00:06:33,984
A set J of indices is a subset D of A. Its neighbour set is the union of the sets Q i for i in J.

34
00:06:34,434 --> 00:06:49,026
So Hall's Theorem becomes Hall's S D R Theorem. The sets have an S D R if and only if, for every set J of indices, the union of the Q i for i in J has at least J elements.

35
00:06:49,476 --> 00:07:06,436
Hall's Theorem also tells us when there is a perfect matching. Corollary 8.6.1: a bipartite graph has a perfect matching if and only if the two sides have the same size and every subset D of A has at least D neighbours.

36
00:07:06,886 --> 00:07:16,657
Proof. A matching has the same number of ends in A and in B. If the sides have different sizes, no matching saturates both sides.

37
00:07:17,107 --> 00:07:34,707
If the sides have the same size, a matching saturates every vertex if and only if it saturates A. Hall's Theorem finishes the proof. The graph of Figure 8.10 has 4 plus 4 vertices, and the blue matching is perfect.

38
00:07:35,157 --> 00:07:48,597
Theorem 8.6.2: if G is a k regular bipartite graph with k at least 1, then G has a perfect matching. The figure shows a 3 regular bipartite graph.

39
00:07:49,047 --> 00:08:04,215
Proof. Every edge has one end in A and one end in B. So the degree sum over A equals the degree sum over B. That is k times A equals k times B. k is not zero, so A and B have the same size.

40
00:08:04,665 --> 00:08:14,755
Now take a subset D of A. Each vertex of D has k edges, so there are k times D edges at D. Each of them ends in N of D.

41
00:08:15,205 --> 00:08:33,040
These edges are only some of the edges at N of D. So k times D is at most the degree sum over N of D, which is k times N of D. In the example, D is 1, 2: the 6 red edges are part of the 12 edges at N of D.

42
00:08:33,490 --> 00:08:42,898
Divide by k. So N of D is at least D, for every D. By Corollary 8.6.1, G has a perfect matching.

43
00:08:43,348 --> 00:08:50,473
Here is one, in blue. The proof never uses that edges are distinct, so it also works for multigraphs.

44
00:08:50,923 --> 00:09:10,273
Here is a practice question from the notes, Problem Set 8.6 number 7. A deck of 52 cards lies in 4 rows and 13 columns. Prove that we can choose 13 cards, one in each column, with no two cards of the same value. Pause the video and try it.

45
00:09:10,723 --> 00:09:27,149
Make a bipartite graph. A is the set of 13 columns. B is the set of 13 values, ace to king. Each card gives one edge, from its column to its value. Two cards of the same value in one column give a multiple edge.

46
00:09:27,599 --> 00:09:40,506
Each column holds 4 cards, so each column vertex has degree 4. Each value appears on 4 cards, so each value vertex has degree 4. The graph is 4 regular.

47
00:09:40,956 --> 00:09:55,825
By Theorem 8.6.2, which also holds for multigraphs, the graph has a perfect matching. Each matching edge is a card. The perfect matching gives one card in each column, and each value once.

48
00:09:56,275 --> 00:10:05,321
For this random layout, the green cards are one such choice. A python check confirms 13 columns and 13 different values.

49
00:10:05,771 --> 00:10:22,027
One more practice question, Problem Set 8.6 number 3. G is bipartite, M is a matching, and D is a subset of A. Prove that M has at most A minus D plus N of D edges. Pause the video and try it.

50
00:10:22,477 --> 00:10:35,063
Split the edges of M into two types. First, the edges with an end in D. Their other ends are in N of D, and they are distinct. So there are at most N of D such edges.

51
00:10:35,513 --> 00:10:45,049
Second, the other edges of M. Their ends in A are in A minus D, and they are distinct. So there are at most A minus D such edges.

52
00:10:45,499 --> 00:10:50,598
Add the two counts. M has at most A minus D plus N of D edges.

53
00:10:51,048 --> 00:11:09,480
On Figure 8.6 with D equal to 1, 2, 5, the bound is 5 minus 3 plus 2, which is 4. Our matching has 4 edges, so the bound proves that it is maximum. Problem 4 of the same set says that the best D always gives the maximum.

54
00:11:09,930 --> 00:11:24,330
An edge k colouring of a graph G gives each edge one of k colours, so that two edges with a common end get different colours. Figure 8.11 of the notes shows an edge 3 colouring of this graph.

55
00:11:24,780 --> 00:11:31,521
Look at the edges of one colour. At each vertex there is at most one of them. So they form a matching.

56
00:11:31,971 --> 00:11:34,190
The same is true for the second colour.

57
00:11:34,640 --> 00:11:36,261
And for the third colour.

58
00:11:36,711 --> 00:11:48,082
So an edge k colouring is the same as a split of the edges of G into k matchings. The notes draw the colours with line styles; here we use real colours.

59
00:11:48,532 --> 00:12:00,799
First, a lower bound on the number of colours. At a vertex v, the deg v edges all need different colours. So k must be at least delta, the maximum degree of G.

60
00:12:01,249 --> 00:12:14,859
This lower bound is not always enough. The triangle K 3 has maximum degree 2. But any two of its edges share an end, so it needs 3 colours. This is Problem Set 8.8 number 1.

61
00:12:15,309 --> 00:12:27,896
For bipartite graphs, delta colours are always enough. This is Theorem 8.7.1: a bipartite graph with maximum degree delta has an edge delta colouring.

62
00:12:28,346 --> 00:12:46,799
The proof uses Lemma 8.7.2: a bipartite graph with at least one edge has a matching that saturates every vertex of maximum degree. In a regular graph, every vertex has maximum degree. So this lemma includes Theorem 8.6.2.

63
00:12:47,249 --> 00:13:15,772
Here is the proof of Lemma 8.7.2. Let K be the set of vertices of maximum degree. Among all maximum matchings, choose M so that it leaves as few vertices of K unsaturated as possible. Suppose some vertex of K is not saturated. By swapping the names A and B, we can say it is in A. Do the X Y construction, but let X 0 be only the unsaturated vertices of A intersect K.

64
00:13:16,222 --> 00:13:44,062
Claim: every vertex w of X has degree delta. Suppose w has smaller degree. P of w has even length. Swap its edges. The size of the matching stays the same, so it is still maximum. The start of the path is now saturated, and it is in K. Only w becomes unsaturated, and w is not in K. So fewer vertices of K are unsaturated. That contradicts the choice of M.

65
00:13:44,512 --> 00:13:56,053
M is a maximum matching, so there is no augmenting path. So every vertex u of Y is saturated, and by the construction its matching edge goes to X.

66
00:13:56,503 --> 00:14:08,386
Each vertex of Y has a matching edge to its own vertex of X. X also contains at least one unsaturated vertex. So X has more vertices than Y.

67
00:14:08,836 --> 00:14:17,199
As in Lemma 8.3.2, there is no edge from X to B minus Y. So all neighbours of X are in Y.

68
00:14:17,649 --> 00:14:37,489
Now count. Delta times Y is less than delta times X. Every vertex of X has degree delta, so delta times X is the degree sum over X. All these edges end in Y, so this is at most the degree sum over Y. And each degree in Y is at most delta.

69
00:14:37,939 --> 00:14:46,579
So delta times Y is less than delta times Y. This is a contradiction. So M saturates every vertex of K.

70
00:14:47,029 --> 00:15:07,253
Now the proof of Theorem 8.7.1, by induction on delta. If delta is 0, there are no edges, and 0 colours are enough. Now let delta be m, at least 1, and assume the result for smaller delta. In the example, m is 3. The red vertices have degree 3.

71
00:15:07,703 --> 00:15:18,049
By Lemma 8.7.2, some matching M saturates every vertex of degree m. Give its edges a new colour, blue, and delete them.

72
00:15:18,499 --> 00:15:38,275
Every vertex of degree m lost one edge. So the rest of the graph, H, has maximum degree m minus 1. By the induction hypothesis, H has an edge colouring with m minus 1 colours. With blue, G has m colours. The red vertices now have degree 2.

73
00:15:38,725 --> 00:15:46,000
Apply the lemma again. This matching saturates the vertices of degree 2. Colour it orange and delete it.

74
00:15:46,450 --> 00:15:51,869
Now every vertex has degree at most 1. The red vertices have degree 1.

75
00:15:52,319 --> 00:16:00,361
The rest is a matching. Colour it purple. Three matchings give an edge 3 colouring. The proof is also an algorithm.

76
00:16:00,811 --> 00:16:20,673
Section 8.8 applies this to timetables. Here is a small example. Instructors P, Q and R teach courses C 1, C 2 and C 3. Each class is one edge, from its instructor to its course. P teaches two sections of C 1, so there are two edges.

77
00:16:21,123 --> 00:16:42,093
In one time slot, an instructor teaches at most one class, and two sections of one course do not run together. So the classes in one slot form a matching. Slots are colours. By Theorem 8.7.1, the fewest slots is delta. Course C 1 has 4 sections, so delta is 4.

78
00:16:42,543 --> 00:16:52,869
Here is an edge 4 colouring, found by search. Each colour is one slot. Three slots are impossible, because C 1 needs 4 different slots.

79
00:16:53,319 --> 00:17:14,801
Now add a room limit: at most m classes in one slot. With q classes, we need at least q over m slots. Theorem 8.8.1 says: if G has an edge k colouring, and q is at most k times m, then G has an edge k colouring that uses each colour at most m times.

80
00:17:15,251 --> 00:17:37,225
Proof. Suppose a colour, red, is used at least m plus 1 times. If every other colour is used at least m times, then q is at least m plus 1 plus m times k minus 1, which is more than k times m. That is false. So some colour, blue, is used at most m minus 1 times.

81
00:17:37,675 --> 00:18:00,288
Take only the red and blue edges. Each vertex has at most one red and one blue edge. So each component is a path or a cycle with alternating colours, and a cycle has equal numbers. Red has more edges than blue, so some component is a path with more red edges. It is an augmenting path for the blue matching.

82
00:18:00,738 --> 00:18:17,762
Swap the colours on this path. Red loses one edge. Blue gains one, so it is used at most m times. Repeat until every colour is used at most m times. In the example, red goes from 4 to 3 and blue from 2 to 3.

83
00:18:18,212 --> 00:18:37,540
Theorems 8.7.1 and 8.8.1 together give Corollary 8.8.2. A bipartite graph has an edge k colouring that uses each colour at most m times if and only if delta is at most k and q is at most k times m.

84
00:18:37,990 --> 00:18:43,067
So the fewest slots is the larger of delta and q over m, rounded up.

85
00:18:43,517 --> 00:18:51,752
In our timetable, q is 9 and delta is 4. With 3 rooms, q over m is 3. So 4 slots are enough.

86
00:18:52,202 --> 00:19:09,802
With 2 rooms, 9 over 2 rounded up is 5. So we need 5 slots. Here is a schedule with 5 slots and at most 2 classes in each. A search confirms that 4 slots with 2 rooms is impossible: 4 times 2 is only 8.

87
00:19:10,252 --> 00:19:26,188
To recap. Hall's Theorem: a matching saturates A if and only if every subset D of A has at least D neighbours. We proved it from König's Theorem. For sets, it says when a system of distinct representatives exists.

88
00:19:26,638 --> 00:19:35,406
A perfect matching needs equal sides and Hall's condition. Every k regular bipartite graph has a perfect matching.

89
00:19:35,856 --> 00:19:55,909
An edge colouring is a split into matchings. A bipartite graph needs only delta colours: peel off one matching for each colour. For a timetable with at most m classes per slot, the fewest slots is the larger of delta and q over m, rounded up. This completes chapter 8.
